[{"category":"section","location":"concepts/simulation/#Simulation","page":"Simulation","text":"","title":"Simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/ising_1d/#Ising-Model-1D-Chain","page":"1D Ising Model","text":"","title":"Ising Model 1D Chain"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/algorithms/#Algorithms","page":"Algorithms","text":"Descriptions of the different algorithms that can be used in UnitCellMC.jl","title":"Algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/algorithms/#Metropolis-Hastings","page":"Algorithms","text":"The Metropolis-Hastings algorithm is a single-spin-flip algorithm, which sweeps through the lattice and updates sites one at a time.\n\nA Monte-Carlo step is as follows.\n\nSelect a site i on the lattice.\nPick a new value for the site\nIsing model: s_i = +1 rightarrow s_i = -1 or s_i = -1 rightarrow s_i = +1\nXY model: s_i = theta_i in 0 2pi) rightarrow s_i = phi_i in 0 2pi), where phi is a new random angle\nCalculate the change in energy Delta E\nIsing model: Delta E = 2 sum_j J_ijs_i s_j + 2 h s_i,  where j is sites that are bonded with site i, and J_ij is the interaction strength of that bond. h is the magnetic field strength.\nXY model: Delta E = -sum_j J_ij cos(phi_i-theta_j) - cos(theta_i - theta_j) - h cos(phi_i)-cos(theta_i).\nDraw a random number rin01\nAccept the update with probability textmin(1e^-betaDelta E), where beta=frac1T.","title":"Metropolis-Hastings"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/measurements/#Measurements","page":"Measurements","text":"","title":"Measurements"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/state/#States","page":"State","text":"","title":"States"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/ising_2d/#Ising-Model-2D-Square-Lattice","page":"2D Ising Model","text":"","title":"Ising Model 2D Square Lattice"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/geometry/#Geometry","page":"Geometry","text":"","title":"Geometry"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/geometry/#Unit-Cell","page":"Geometry","text":"The unit cell is the smallest repeating block which builds your lattice.","title":"Unit Cell"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/geometry/#Lattice-Vectors","page":"Geometry","text":"The lattice vectors describe the direction and edges where the the unit cell is replicated across.","title":"Lattice Vectors"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/geometry/#Example-lattices","page":"Geometry","text":"","title":"Example lattices"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/geometry/#1D-Chain","page":"Geometry","text":"","title":"1D Chain"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/geometry/#Square","page":"Geometry","text":"","title":"Square"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/geometry/#Kagome","page":"Geometry","text":"","title":"Kagome"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/geometry/#Honeycomb","page":"Geometry","text":"","title":"Honeycomb"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Simulation","page":"Simulation","text":"\"\"\"     SimulationParameters(β, nthermalization, nsteps, n_unmeasured)\n\nParameters controlling a Monte Carlo simulation.","title":"Simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Arguments","page":"Simulation","text":"β: Inverse temperature.\nn_thermalization: Number of thermalization steps.\nn_steps: Number of Monte Carlo steps.\nn_unmeasured: Number of steps between measurements.\n\n\"\"\" struct SimulationParameters     β::Float64     nthermalization::Int     nsteps::Int     n_unmeasured::Int end","title":"Arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"api/measurements/#Measurements","page":"Measurements","text":"","title":"Measurements"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/models/#Models","page":"Models","text":"","title":"Models"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/models/#Ising-Model","page":"Models","text":"The Ising model is described by the following Hamiltonian: H=-sum_langle i j rangle J_ijs_i s_j - sum_i h_i s_i\n\nWhere J_ij is the interaction strength between site i and site j, s_i is the value of site i (pm1), and h_i is the external field strength at site i.","title":"Ising Model"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Getting-Started","page":"Getting Started","text":"","title":"Getting Started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Installation","page":"Getting Started","text":"Install UnitCellMC using Julia's package manager.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Basic-Workflow","page":"Getting Started","text":"A typical simulation consists of several components:\n\nDefine a lattice geometry.\nDefine a model.\nDefine the simulation parameters.\nSelect a Monte Carlo algorithm.\nRun the simulation.\nAnalyze the measurements.","title":"Basic Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"api/geometry/#Geometry","page":"Geometry","text":"","title":"Geometry"},{"category":"section","location":"todo_in_progress/#To-Do-/-In-Progress","page":"TODO / In Progress","text":"In the future we'd like to add some further functionality to this package.","title":"To Do / In Progress"},{"category":"section","location":"todo_in_progress/#Models","page":"TODO / In Progress","text":"XY Model\nHeisenberg Model\nClock Model\nPotts Model","title":"Models"},{"category":"section","location":"todo_in_progress/#Algorithms","page":"TODO / In Progress","text":"Wolff Cluster\nSwendsen-Wang Cluster\nGlauber/Heatbath","title":"Algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"todo_in_progress/#Functionality","page":"TODO / In Progress","text":"Define J by bond rather than for the entire lattice\nTwo-point correlation functions\nFile outputs (CSV)\nCheckpointing (JLD2)","title":"Functionality"},{"category":"section","location":"#UnitCellMC.jl","page":"Home","text":"Monte Carlo simulations of classical lattice models on arbitrary lattice geometries.","title":"UnitCellMC.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Overview","page":"Home","text":"UnitCellMC.jl is designed to provide a flexible framework for simulating classical lattice models. Currently, it supports the Ising model with the Metropolis algorithm, with further extensions to come in the future.\n\nThe geometry of the system is separated from the physical model, allowing the same simulation framework to be used with different lattice geometries.","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"#Acknowledgements","page":"Home","text":"This package makes use of LatticeUtilities.jl, and takes some design inspiration from SmoQyDQMC.jl.","title":"Acknowledgements"},{"category":"section","location":"api/algorithms/#Algorithms","page":"Algorithms","text":"","title":"Algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"api/algorithms/#Metropolis-Hastings","page":"Algorithms","text":"","title":"Metropolis-Hastings"},{"category":"section","location":"api/models/#Models","page":"Models","text":"","title":"Models"}]
